From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] The compile job waits for an idle device, not for a charger

setRequiresCharging(true) stands in for "do not strand the owner". On a phone it
costs nothing, because a phone is charged every night and "idle and charging"
comes round daily. A Kindle runs for weeks on one charge, so the condition is
almost never true, and this job never runs -- which matters more here than the
name suggests, because it is the only thing that ever compiles an application
the owner installed, or writes a reference profile for one. pw3.mk has said so
since 2026-10-20; what it could not say was what that costs.

It costs half the time it takes to open a book. Same device, same account, same
shelf of a dozen books, started by tapping the icon, A A B B A A with three
minutes of settling after each compile (2026-10-28, V91):

    speed-profile   99.8 / 88.9 / 104.8 / 89.0 s     CPU 77.6 / 69.7 / 74.3 / 70.5
    speed           55.4 / 47.3 s                    CPU 30.8 / 29.5

Wall 95.6 s against 51.4 s, process CPU 73.0 against 30.2, and the groups do not
overlap. Garbage collection barely moves, so the 43 seconds are bytecode running
in the interpreter. The closing rounds come back to 89-105 s, so the machine did
not drift; and the speed rounds followed 1279 s of dex2oat at full tilt, which
biases against the result rather than for it.

The condition this replaces it with is what the charger was standing in for.
setRequiresDeviceIdle(true) stays: the work still waits until the owner has put
the device down.

Two things make this safe to run off a charger, and both are elsewhere:
frameworks/native patch 0009 makes a compile keep the code it is replacing until
the new code is ready, so a cancelled run costs nothing but the work; and
installd already drops dex2oat to nice 10, which leaves a foreground application
about nine tenths of this single core if the owner picks the device up anyway.

Not run on the device yet when this patch was written.

--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/pm/BackgroundDexOptService.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/pm/BackgroundDexOptService.java
@@ -117,7 +117,14 @@
         // those with fresh profiling data.
         js.schedule(new JobInfo.Builder(JOB_IDLE_OPTIMIZE, sDexoptServiceName)
                     .setRequiresDeviceIdle(true)
-                    .setRequiresCharging(true)
+                    // PW3: "on a charger" is a stand-in for "do not strand the
+                    // owner", and on a phone it costs nothing, because a phone
+                    // is charged every night. A Kindle runs for weeks on one
+                    // charge, so the condition is almost never true and this
+                    // job -- the only thing that ever compiles an application
+                    // the owner installed, or writes a reference profile --
+                    // never runs. What it stands for, said directly:
+                    .setRequiresBatteryNotLow(true)
                     .setPeriodic(IDLE_OPTIMIZATION_PERIOD)
                     .build());
 
